<section class="section">
  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Every repo under Cubic Echo should have a site at
    <code>cubicecho.github.io/&lt;repo&gt;/</code> — even the small libraries,
    whose whole story is a paragraph and an install line. cubesite is what makes
    that cheap: a scaffold that writes the site, a stylesheet they all share, and
    one Pages workflow that is the same file in every repo.
  </p>

  <ul class="card-grid">
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>theme</h3>
        <p>
          <a class="card-link" href="{{ '/theme/cubesite.css' | url }}">cubesite.css</a> —
          the colors, type, header, cards and prose every project site is built
          from, in both light and dark.
        </p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>brand</h3>
        <p>The mark, the wordmark, the tokens, and the few rules for using them.
          <span class="card-actions"><a class="card-site" href="{{ '/brand/' | url }}">see them ↗</a></span>
        </p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>template</h3>
        <p>A one-page Eleventy site, already branded, with the TODOs marked. Fill
          in three of them and it is a real site.</p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>pages.yml</h3>
        <p>Build <code>site/</code>, refuse to publish an empty one, deploy. The
          same file in every repo, so a fix travels.</p>
      </article>
    </li>
  </ul>
