<section class="section">
  <h2>Why</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The same argument as <a href="https://github.com/cubicecho/cubeui" rel="noopener">cubeui</a>,
      one layer out. Four repos had a site before this one existed —
      <code>mcp-router</code>, <code>mcp-skills-manager</code>, <code>auto-cal</code>,
      <code>cubeui</code> — and each had written its own stylesheet, its own
      layout, its own header. They do not look like the same org, because nothing
      made them. One of them wears a 🔀 as its logo.
    </p>
    <p>
      The other half is coverage. Most repos had no site at all, so the only
      public face of something like <code>agent-core</code> was a README in
      GitHub's chrome — fine for a reader already on GitHub, nothing for a link
      in a chat or a search result. A one-page site is cheap. It was just never
      anybody's job.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <h2>Get started</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <pre class="code"><code>git clone https://github.com/cubicecho/cubesite
cd cubesite &amp;&amp; npm install

# add a site to a repo that has none
node scripts/scaffold.mjs ../&lt;repo&gt;

# re-brand a repo that already has one
node scripts/scaffold.mjs ../&lt;repo&gt; --theme

# which repos are still missing a site?
npm run audit</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p class="readme-link">
    Full documentation lives in the project README:
    <a href="{{ site.repo }}#readme" rel="noopener">{{ site.repo }}</a>
  </p>
